\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Effect of the commute overlay on the hourly OD matrices. Left column: base gravity model ($\lambda = 0$). Centre column: model with commute overlay ($\lambda > 0$). Right column: difference (centre $-$ left). Top row: morning peak hour ($h = 8$). Bottom row: evening peak hour ($h = 17$). Red cells in the difference maps indicate flows amplified by the overlay, bylue cells indicate flows attenuated. The AM overlay concentrates demand on residential$\to $employment corridors, the PM overlay reverses this pattern.}}{18}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:commute_overlay}{{6}{18}{Effect of the commute overlay on the hourly OD matrices. Left column: base gravity model ($\lambda = 0$). Centre column: model with commute overlay ($\lambda > 0$). Right column: difference (centre $-$ left). Top row: morning peak hour ($h = 8$). Bottom row: evening peak hour ($h = 17$). Red cells in the difference maps indicate flows amplified by the overlay, bylue cells indicate flows attenuated. The AM overlay concentrates demand on residential$\to $employment corridors, the PM overlay reverses this pattern}{figure.6}{}}
